HLTH-VEC-RES-002-RC18	HLTH-VEC-RES-002	Misconfigured TTL or lifecycle policies	misconfigured-ttl-or-lifecycle-policies	Namespaces or per-tenant collections are never expired or consolidated, so historical tenants accumulate indefinitely. Vendor-Specific Notes: - Pinecone: Namespaces are the multi-tenant unit; very high namespace counts per index increase routing and metadata load, so designs should favor fewer namespaces plus metadata filters. - Milvus: Excessive partitions used as “namespaces” stress etcd and segment management; Partition Key is the recommended mechanism instead of many partitions. - Qdrant: Collection-per-tenant or heavy shard-per-collection usage increases optimizer and routing cost; use payload filters and on-disk payload where appropriate. - Weaviate: Multi-tenancy with many tenants maps to many shards; recommended to balance tenants per class and avoid explosion of small, idle tenants. - pgvector: Table-per-tenant mimics namespace sprawl; catalog and autovacuum overhead scale with table count. - MongoDB Atlas Vector Search: Per-tenant collections and indexes multiply the work of the vector search engine and the underlying cluster. - Elasticsearch/OpenSearch: Index-per-tenant is explicitly discouraged; high index counts lead to large cluster state and routing overhead. - Redis VSS: Key/slot distribution issues emerge with many small prefix-based “namespaces,” affecting latency and persistence file sizes.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.639172	2026-02-23 20:18:30.639173
