HLTH-VEC-MNT-002-RC20	HLTH-VEC-MNT-002	Replication lag of schema changes	replication-lag-of-schema-changes	Primary’s schema update is ahead of replicas; clients routed to replicas encounter old dimensions or index definitions. Vendor-Specific Notes: - Pinecone: Vector dimension is fixed per index; any change requires deleting and recreating the index with the new dimension. - Milvus: Vector field `dim` is immutable; you must drop/recreate the collection (and re-ingest data) to change dimensions. - Qdrant: Collection config declares vector size; inserts must match exactly, or they are rejected. - Weaviate: Class vectorizer and dimensions are tied to schema; changing vectorizer often implies creating a new class or reimporting data. - pgvector: Column types like `vector(1536)` enforce exact dimensions; ALTER TABLE is required for a dimension change. - MongoDB Atlas Vector Search: Vector index definitions include `dimensions`; all vectors must match that length. - Elasticsearch/OpenSearch: Dense vector fields must use the dimension declared in the mapping; changing requires a new index and reindex. - Redis VSS: RediSearch vector index DIM setting must match all stored vectors; mismatches cause index errors.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.639207	2026-02-23 20:18:30.639207
HLTH-VEC-MNT-001-RC18	HLTH-VEC-MNT-001	Network bottlenecks for distributed shard movement	network-bottlenecks-for-distributed-shard-movement	Compaction that involves shard relocation or merging across nodes is limited by network throughput, extending maintenance windows indefinitely. Vendor-Specific Notes: - Milvus: Compaction and WAL/binlog behavior governed by DataCoord and configuration; stale etcd state and binlog thresholds are common sources of stalls. - Qdrant: Background optimizer handles segment merging and vacuuming; its thresholds and segment-count settings must match ingest patterns. - Weaviate: Background maintenance over many shards/tenants can extend compaction times; scaling pattern and shard counts influence behavior. - pgvector/PostgreSQL: Autovacuum and REINDEX are the principal compaction-like tools; misconfigured autovacuum or low maintenance memory cause bloat. - MongoDB Atlas Vector Search: Compaction and cleanup are tied to replication health and cluster resource allocation. - Elasticsearch/OpenSearch: Force merge and ILM settings must be tuned to avoid long-running merges on large vector indices. - Pinecone, Chroma, Redis: Compaction is mostly automatic or implicit (e.g., RDB/AOF rewrite in Redis); issues arise when persistence or background maintenance is constrained or blocked.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.63919	2026-02-23 20:18:30.63919
