PERF-SQL-LC-004-RC09	PERF-SQL-LC-004	Insufficient ITL (Oracle) or block header space	insufficient-itl-oracle-or-block-header-space	Limited interest transaction list slots force concurrent transactions to wait for slots to become available.	\N	{oracle}	{"mysql": "Buffer pool mutex contention on high concurrency workloads. InnoDB adaptive hash index latch contention with many lookups. Metadata lock hash table contention with many DDL operations. InnoDB lock hash table contention with many concurrent locks.", "oracle": "Latch free waits indicate memory structure contention. Cache buffer latch contention (CBC latch) occurs on frequently-accessed blocks. Redo log buffer latch contention during high DML. Free list latch contention when allocating new extents. Library cache latch contention during heavy parsing. ITL contention manifests as `enq: TX - allocate ITL entry` waits (technically enqueue, not latch, but similar impact).", "mariadb": "Buffer pool mutex contention on high concurrency workloads. InnoDB adaptive hash index latch contention with many lookups. Metadata lock hash table contention with many DDL operations. InnoDB lock hash table contention with many concurrent locks.", "sqlserver": "PAGELATCH_EX waits on page modifications, PAGELATCH_SH on page reads. Hot page inserts (sequential keys) cause extreme contention. SUPER_LATCH promotion helps but can fail with warning. Allocation page contention in TEMPDB for temp table creation. `OPTIMIZE_FOR_SEQUENTIAL_KEY` index option can mitigate last-page contention.", "postgresql": "LWLocks (lightweight locks) protect internal memory structures. Buffer pin contention occurs on frequently-accessed pages. Predicate lock memory pressure in SERIALIZABLE transactions causes false positives. `max_pred_locks_per_transaction` controls predicate lock memory allocation."}	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640219	2026-02-23 20:18:30.64022
PERF-SQL-LC-005-RC15	PERF-SQL-LC-005	XID wraparound vulnerability (PostgreSQL)	xid-wraparound-vulnerability-postgresql	Table must be vacuumed to prevent XID wraparound, causing aggressive autovacuum that blocks long-running transactions.	\N	{postgresql}	{"mysql": "No built-in transaction timeout (relies on application timeout). `max_execution_time` hint controls query timeout. InnoDB undo log for long-running transactions can prevent purge of deleted rows, slowing down other transactions. No automatic transaction timeout for idle connections (must be handled by application pooling layer).", "oracle": "`max_idle_blocker_time` parameter can kill idle blocking sessions. Long-running transactions prevent undo segment reuse, eventually causing \\"ORA-01555: snapshot too old\\" errors. UNDO_RETENTION parameter extends undo retention. Flashback can help recover from accidental long-transaction isolation issues. `DB_TIME` metric tracks actual transaction time in AWR reports.", "mariadb": "No built-in transaction timeout (relies on application timeout). `max_execution_time` hint controls query timeout. InnoDB undo log for long-running transactions can prevent purge of deleted rows, slowing down other transactions. No automatic transaction timeout for idle connections (must be handled by application pooling layer).", "sqlserver": "No transaction timeout built-in (must implement at application level). SNAPSHOT isolation allows reads but not writes during long transactions. Resource semaphores prevent memory pressure from long-running queries. Tempdb allocation tracking can reveal extent of undo needed for rolled-back long transactions.", "postgresql": "`statement_timeout` kills individual statements. `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` kills idle open transactions (very helpful). Default `deadlock_timeout` of 1 second will also log long lock waits. Vacuum must occur before XID wraparound (200 million transactions), forcing aggressive autovacuum. `pg_stat_activity` shows `xact_start` vs `query_start` to identify transaction duration."}	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.64022	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640221
PERF-SQL-VS-001-RC14	PERF-SQL-VS-001	Recovery/crash recovery running indefinitely	recoverycrash-recovery-running-indefinitely	Incomplete crash recovery or replica recovery keeps minimal transaction IDs frozen, preventing normal VACUUM operations. **Architectural Context:** PostgreSQL uses a 32-bit unsigned integer for transaction IDs (XIDs). While this provides ~4 billion possible values, the MVCC visibility system divides the XID space into past and future: anything beyond 2^31 transactions from the current position wraps around. Rows store their creation (xmin) and deletion (xmax) XIDs; if wraparound occurs without freezing, old rows suddenly appear "in the future" and become invisible, corrupting data visibility. The VACUUM process freezes transaction IDs by replacing old xmin/xmax values with a special FrozenXID, making them visible regardless of wraparound. However, VACUUM cannot freeze rows modified by transactions that are still active or visible—hence it requires all old transaction IDs to be completed and their changes committed.	\N	{postgresql}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640229	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640229
