PERF-SQL-VS-002-RC15	PERF-SQL-VS-002	System catalog TOAST bloat (pg_description, pg_attribute comments)	system-catalog-toast-bloat-pg-description-pg-attribute-comments	Comments on columns and objects stored in TOAST catalog tables can accumulate dead versions if frequently modified, slowing catalog queries. **Architectural Context:** PostgreSQL's TOAST (The Oversized-Attribute Storage Technique) solves the problem of fitting large values into fixed 8 KB pages. When a tuple exceeds `TOAST_TUPLE_THRESHOLD` (default 2 KB), PostgreSQL attempts compression, then out-of-line storage in a separate TOAST table. TOAST tables are stored identically to regular tables—with MVCC, autovacuum, and the same bloat mechanics. Each column's TOAST strategy (EXTENDED, MAIN, EXTERNAL, PLAIN) controls whether compression and/or out-of-line storage is attempted. The TOAST mechanism is essential for correctness but creates a secondary storage space that bloats independently if autovacuum cannot keep pace with updates. Unlike table bloat (which is often survivable with slower queries), unchecked TOAST bloat can consume enormous disk space with minimal query-visible benefit, as the large values are accessed infrequently compared to TOAST pointer maintenance overhead.	\N	{postgresql}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640232	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640233
