PERF-SQL-VS-003-RC14	PERF-SQL-VS-003	Mergeout memory constraints	mergeout-memory-constraints	If Tuple Mover resource pool MEMORYSIZE is too low, mergeout operations fail or are blocked waiting for memory, preventing container cleanup. **Architectural Context:** Vertica's hybrid architecture uses ROS (Read-Optimized Store) for disk-resident data and WOS (Write-Optimized Store) for in-memory temporary storage. The Tuple Mover background process continuously moves data from WOS to ROS (moveout) and merges multiple ROS containers into larger containers (mergeout). Mergeout is crucial because many small ROS containers degrade query performance—each container requires separate decompression, more file handles, and more intermediate merge operations in queries scanning the data. Vertica enforces a hard limit of 1024 ROS containers per projection per node to prevent metadata explosion and query fan-in explosion. When this limit is reached, Vertica refuses all new COPY/INSERT operations and returns ROS pushback errors. The strata algorithm groups containers by size and promotes them through exponential size tiers; containers must progress through strata, and mergeout operations are carefully scheduled to minimize re-touching of data. Long-running mergeouts on wide tables or insufficient mergeout parallelism can cause the pipeline to stall, allowing new containers from loads to accumulate faster than old containers are merged away.	\N	{vertica}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640233	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640234
PERF-VEC-MEM-002-RC04	PERF-VEC-MEM-002	Segment-level fragmentation accumulation	segment-level-fragmentation-accumulation	Small segments created during frequent upserts not automatically merged; segment count configuration misaligned (too many small segments instead of fewer large ones), preventing consolidation.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	{"milvus": "Manual trigger of compaction via compaction API needed; memory bloat accumulates in sealed segments; delegator memory imbalance preventing offload", "qdrant": "Vacuum optimizer activation criteria (deleted_threshold, vacuum_min_vector_number) must be tuned per workload; manual optimization API available but not auto-triggered", "weaviate": "PQ training data not auto-cleared; manual quantization rebuild required to free old codebook memory", "postgresql": "Vacuum (VACUUM ANALYZE) required to reclaim bloat; autovacuum daemon tuning critical; no streaming compaction"}	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640249	2026-02-23 20:18:30.64025
PERF-VEC-IDX-004-RC08	PERF-VEC-IDX-004	Skewed data undetected	skewed-data-undetected	Statistics assume uniform distribution but actual data is skewed, making cost estimates unreliable and leading to suboptimal query or search plans.	\N	{chromadb,milvus,qdrant,weaviate}	{"redis": "Single-threaded statistics collection; high query volume blocks statistics updates. Background optimization thread handles statistics incrementally", "milvus": "ANALYZE command not exposed; statistics maintained through segment optimization. Collection compaction updates statistics implicitly", "qdrant": "On-disk storage separates statistics from queries; can cause stale stats on frequently accessed collections. indexing_threshold changes affect statistics interpretation", "mongodb": "Statistics automatically collected; manual stats refresh available via compactCollection. Index statistics specific to search nodes; main node statistics different", "weaviate": "Statistics implicit in HNSW layer structure; no explicit statistics maintenance. Dynamic ef adjustment based on real-time performance implicitly handles stale stats", "postgresql": "ANALYZE command mandatory after index operations. autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor controls frequency. default_statistics_target (0-10000) controls sample size. Manual ANALYZE can be forced anytime without locking"}	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640234	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640235
PERF-SQL-VS-004-RC15	PERF-SQL-VS-004	Tuple Mover not completing moveout fast enough	tuple-mover-not-completing-moveout-fast-enough	Although moveout is separate from WOS capacity, if Tuple Mover is CPU-bound or I/O-bound and cannot complete moveout operations quickly, new loads cannot clear WOS space. **Architectural Context:** Vertica's WOS is an in-memory data store optimized for rapid ingestion of small, frequent loads (trickle loading). The WOS stores data unsorted and uncompressed, allowing writes without the I/O cost of disk access. Vertica automatically moves data from WOS to ROS either when a load completes or when WOS reaches capacity (whichever comes first in moveout), or when WOS becomes full (forced spillover). WOS capacity is fixed at 25% of available system RAM or 2 GB maximum, whichever is smaller. When WOS reaches capacity before moveout completes, subsequent data is spilled to disk (directly written to ROS), bypassing the in-memory fast path. This spillover degrades ingestion performance because: - Spilled data must be written to disk immediately (no memory buffering) - Spilled data is not pre-sorted, causing slower subsequent queries - ROS container count increases (each spill creates a small ROS container) - Query performance degrades due to more containers to scan and merge The WOS spill threshold is reached more frequently under high concurrency or large batch loads, especially if moveout cannot keep pace or if data is loaded with AUTO or DIRECT hints without proper configuration. Unlike queries on spilled data (which is still queryable), WOS spill primarily impacts *ingestion performance*, not query correctness, but it cascades into container count issues if frequent spills occur.	\N	{vertica}	null	\N	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640236	2026-02-23 20:18:30.640236
