PERF-SQL-LC-001-RC01	PERF-SQL-LC-001	Inconsistent lock ordering	inconsistent-lock-ordering	Transactions access the same resources in different orders, creating circular wait conditions (e.g., Transaction A locks Table X then Table Y, while Transaction B locks Table Y then Table X).	\N	{mariadb,mysql,oracle,postgresql,sqlserver,vertica,informix}	{"mysql": "InnoDB immediately detects deadlock cycles via wait-for graph. Metadata locks can block DDL statements and queries simultaneously. Lock wait timeout (`innodb_lock_wait_timeout`, default 50 seconds) may occur before deadlock detection completes in some scenarios.", "oracle": "TX (transaction) enqueue mode 6 detects row lock conflicts. Foreign key constraint violations cause TM (DML) enqueue waits. ITL (Interested Transaction List) contention can manifest as deadlock-like symptoms. Enqueue wait events can be tuned with `DML_LOCKS` parameter.", "mariadb": "InnoDB immediately detects deadlock cycles via wait-for graph. Metadata locks can block DDL statements and queries simultaneously. Lock wait timeout (`innodb_lock_wait_timeout`, default 50 seconds) may occur before deadlock detection completes in some scenarios.", "sqlserver": "Deadlock detection runs every ~5 seconds by default. Reader-writer deadlocks and parallelism-related deadlocks are common. Parallel query worker threads can deadlock with each other. Key lookup deadlocks occur when non-covering indexes force clustered index access.", "postgresql": "Deadlock detection uses lazy evaluation with `deadlock_timeout` parameter (default 1 second). Predicate locks in SERIALIZABLE isolation can cause false positives. Foreign key constraints without indexes on referencing columns trigger TM enqueue-like waits."}	{"sqlserver": "access accessing acquiring active address alphabetically always analyze analyzes apply are automatically before buffer can captures cause causing child circular classic clustered cntr_value common conditions confirm consider control convention co